Make z use its address and stop at the ends of the buffer.
z always worked from the current line, ignoring any address, and passed
a fixed 22-line window to printLines, which rejects a range that leaves
the buffer. So "5z" printed the wrong lines, and near either end of the
buffer z printed nothing but "bad line range for print". Take the window
from the addressed line, which defaults to the current line, and print
the part of it that lies inside the buffer.

---
 editors/ed.c |   16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/ed.c
+++ b/editors/ed.c
@@ -977,17 +977,27 @@
 			break;
 
 		case 'z':
+			if (bad_nums(num1, num1, "print"))
+				break;
 			switch (*cp) {
 			case '-':
-				printLines(curNum - 21, curNum, FALSE);
+				num2 = num1;
+				num1 -= 21;
 				break;
 			case '.':
-				printLines(curNum - 11, curNum + 10, FALSE);
+				num2 = num1 + 10;
+				num1 -= 11;
 				break;
 			default:
-				printLines(curNum, curNum + 21, FALSE);
+				num2 = num1 + 21;
 				break;
 			}
+			/* Print the part of the window inside the buffer */
+			if (num1 < 1)
+				num1 = 1;
+			if (num2 > lastNum)
+				num2 = lastNum;
+			printLines(num1, num2, FALSE);
 			break;
 
 		case '.':
